 S~S  Software Development Plans versus Publishing Plans

------------------------------------------

   The software named "ssTEA" is added to an App to make it into a "SimpleSal App".

   ssTEA was architected, designed and implemented to not do anything the user can do.
   Said the other way around: if an App's software can do the task, SimpleSal won't volunteer.

   ssTEA will keep Time and then execute your Event function at the Time you requested.

   Publishing the definition of the interface to ssTEA, a so-called API, sets it in stone.

   Development plans are constrained to completing the implementation without breaking anything.

   The expectation is that the SimpleSal software ecosystem will be fairly static once published.

   The SimpleSal Apps published by TSC LLC, written by anyone, will be where all the activity is.

------------------------------------------

   A release is planned implementing changes required to transition from Development to Production.
   TSC LLC calls the transition, big 'D' Development to big 'P' Production, "publishing software".

    * formalize and make canonical all metadata files across all SimpleSal ecosystem releases

    * remove all demo and debug configuration hacks present in the software to support videos

    * incorporate "soft open" feedback from experienced GitHub users and Arduino users

